/* BPT theory sheets: a classmate's exam overview, checked against the syllabus, slides and readings (30 Oct); corrections listed per sheet. */
const BPT_SHEETS=[{"id": "0", "title": "How to use", "html": "<p><strong>BEYOND PLANNING THEORY | FINAL EXAM</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Theory Sheets</strong></p>\n<p>One page per theory, in the order of the exam questions</p>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>What the exam asks (and where it is on every sheet)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Q1 (40 points):</strong> what the author does; <strong>which theory</strong> fits, with <strong>2 quotes</strong>; its <strong>building blocks</strong>; <strong>2 assumptions</strong> on the course framework, each with a quote. → boxes <strong>\"Recognize it\"</strong>, <strong>\"Building blocks\"</strong> and the <strong>framework table</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Q2 (40 points):</strong> name an <strong>alternative theory</strong>, its <strong>building blocks</strong>, its assumptions on <strong>2 dimensions</strong>; why it is relevant; how its assumptions <strong>differ</strong>; what it <strong>helps us understand</strong> that the original does not. → boxes <strong>\"Building blocks\"</strong>, <strong>framework table</strong> and <strong>\"Use it as the alternative\"</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Q3 (20 points):</strong> how the alternative <strong>reformulates the research focus</strong>; which <strong>methods</strong> fit and <strong>why</strong>; <strong>concrete examples</strong>. → box <strong>\"Research focus and methods\"</strong>.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>How to use these sheets</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Your exam notes must be handwritten.</strong> Use these sheets as the master: copy each one by hand onto one page (front of an A4) and keep the same boxes in the same place, so you can find things fast under time pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Same layout on every page.</strong> Top left = how to recognize the theory in the article. Top right = building blocks. Middle = framework. Bottom = alternative (Q2) and methods (Q3).</p>\n<p><strong>Positions are tendencies, not boxes.</strong> Always add a reason from the article (\"… because the author writes that …\"). Use the course framework from the syllabus: four dimensions with their positions: <strong>sources of planning outcomes</strong> (structure ↔ agency); <strong>reproduction and change</strong> (reproduction ↔ transformation; deliberate ↔ emergent); <strong>knowledge and expertise</strong> (universal ↔ situated; monist ↔ pluralist); <strong>normative commitments</strong> (e.g. efficiency, democracy, equity, sustainability, justice). Realism ↔ relativism comes from the knowledge lecture (15 Sept): a useful extra for the knowledge dimension, but not one of the framework positions.</p>\n<p><strong>Analyse, do not judge.</strong> A theory is a lens that tells you what to look at. Changing the theory <strong>changes the question, not the verdict</strong> (the \"category error\" from the urban politics lecture).</p>\n<p><strong>Combinations are allowed.</strong> Q1 says \"theory or combination of theories\". Many articles mix two (for example political economy plus discourse in Gurran &amp; Ruming, urban politics plus commons in Bossuyt &amp; D’Ottaviano). Name the main one and the secondary one.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Contents</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Sheet</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Lectures and readings</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td>Policy analysis and rational comprehensive planning</td>\n<td>L4: Rydin Ch. 2, Scott, Davoudi; (L2 positivism)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td>Rational choice</td>\n<td>L5: Needham, Hindmoor/Olson, Taylor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3</strong></td>\n<td>New institutionalism: the umbrella (what all four share, comparison, quick test)</td>\n<td>L6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3a</strong></td>\n<td>Rational choice institutionalism</td>\n<td>Ostrom, North; (L5 link)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3b</strong></td>\n<td>Sociological institutionalism</td>\n<td>Dembski &amp; Salet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3c</strong></td>\n<td>Historical institutionalism</td>\n<td>Sorensen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3d</strong></td>\n<td>Evolutionary institutionalism</td>\n<td>Moroni; (L3 Hayek, Cozzolino)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td>Political economy</td>\n<td>L7: Harvey, Foglesong, Hyötyläinen &amp; Haila; (L3 Marx)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5</strong></td>\n<td>Urban politics</td>\n<td>L8: Rydin Ch. 6, Bossuyt &amp; D’Ottaviano, Pruijt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>6</strong></td>\n<td>Discourse, power and knowledge</td>\n<td>L9: Raco &amp; Imrie, Gurran &amp; Ruming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>7</strong></td>\n<td>Exam tools: choosing the alternative, methods, answer templates and time plan</td>\n<td>All</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["The course framework (syllabus, section 4.1) has four dimensions: sources of planning outcomes (structure ↔ agency), reproduction and change (reproduction ↔ transformation, deliberate ↔ emergent), knowledge and expertise (universal ↔ situated, monist ↔ pluralist) and normative commitments. Realism ↔ relativism is from the 15 Sept knowledge lecture, not a framework dimension; it is kept on the sheets as an extra row."]}, {"id": "1", "title": "Policy analysis and rational comprehensive planning", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>THEORY</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1</strong></p></td>\n<td><h3>Policy analysis and rational comprehensive planning</h3>\n<p>Planning as a <strong>logical, step by step, scientific</strong> process: an expert planner defines goals, studies options, chooses the best one with <strong>objective evidence</strong>, implements and evaluates. It serves <strong>the public interest</strong> and assumes knowledge can be value free.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 Recognize it: signal words and typical article</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> evidence based, optimal, efficiency, cost benefit analysis (MKBA), indicators, model, forecast, objective, best practice, effectiveness, goals and means, monitoring, evaluation, data driven, policy cycle, implementation gap, \"what works\".</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> proposes or tests a <strong>tool</strong> (index, GIS model, multi criteria evaluation), measures <strong>policy effects</strong>, or gives recommendations to planners on how to decide better.</p>\n<p><strong>• Tell:</strong> the planner/state is the <strong>one rational actor</strong>; problems are taken as <strong>given</strong>; no attention to power or meaning.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 / Q2 Building blocks (name at least 3)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• The rational model</strong> (steps): define problem and goals → generate alternatives → evaluate (CBA) → choose → implement → monitor (Rydin Ch. 2: the governmental model).</p>\n<p><strong>• Public interest:</strong> planning corrects <strong>market failure</strong> and maximizes total welfare, for example through cost benefit analysis: close to the utilitarian Happy City of L1.</p>\n<p><strong>• Positivism:</strong> knowledge is universal, monist, realist and value free (L2, Hall’s \"positivist\" era).</p>\n<p><strong>• Separation of facts and values:</strong> politicians set goals, experts supply means.</p>\n<p><strong>• High modernism (Scott):</strong> seeing like a state, legibility, the master plan \"from above\", standardization, separation of functions (Le Corbusier, Brasília).</p>\n<p><strong>• Evidence based planning (Davoudi):</strong> evidence should inform, not determine; \"rhetoric and reality\".</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Framework dimension</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Position</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Write it like this in Q1 / Q2</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td>Agency (of the planner)</td>\n<td>\"The author assumes the planner can steer outcomes deliberately through rational choices; structural constraints are not discussed.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reproduction ↔ transformation</strong></td>\n<td>Transformation</td>\n<td>\"Planning is seen as a tool that can change the city towards set goals.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deliberate ↔ emergent</strong></td>\n<td>Deliberate</td>\n<td>\"Change is the result of a designed plan or policy, not of many uncoordinated actions.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Universal ↔ situated</strong></td>\n<td>Universal</td>\n<td>\"Knowledge is treated as valid regardless of who produced it: the model or indicator applies everywhere.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monist ↔ pluralist</strong></td>\n<td>Monist</td>\n<td>\"Only expert, scientific knowledge counts; local or lay knowledge is not used.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Realism ↔ relativism</strong></td>\n<td>Realism</td>\n<td>\"The problem is treated as objectively measurable and claims can be tested against data.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normative</strong></td>\n<td>Efficiency, welfare, public interest</td>\n<td>\"Values are implicit: the best option is the most efficient one, presented as neutral.\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q2 Use it as the alternative</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Best against:</strong> discourse, urban politics, political economy, rational choice. All show what the \"neutral\" expert model hides.</p>\n<p><strong>• Use it as the alternative when</strong> the article is very critical and qualitative but never asks whether a policy <strong>works</strong> (e.g. against a pure discourse paper): it reveals <strong>effects and outcomes</strong> that can be measured.</p>\n<p><strong>• Ready sentence:</strong> \"Where the author treats knowledge as situated and contested, a rational comprehensive perspective assumes that problems can be measured objectively and solved through systematic comparison of alternatives, which helps to reveal …\"</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q3 Research focus and methods</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Typical RQ:</strong> \"Which policy option most effectively/efficiently reduces X?\" or \"What is the effect of policy Y on X?\"</p>\n<p><strong>• Methods:</strong> cost benefit analysis, multi criteria evaluation, GIS and spatial modelling, statistics and regression, ex ante / ex post evaluation, surveys, indicators.</p>\n<p><strong>• Why:</strong> the theory assumes an objective reality and one best answer, so it needs <strong>measurable, comparable</strong> data from an <strong>etic</strong> (outsider) view.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Course examples to cite, and do not confuse with</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cite:</strong> Rydin Ch. 2 (governmental model); Scott (high modernism, Brasília, Jacobs as the critique: \"eyes on the street\"); Davoudi (evidence based planning, green belt survives as ideology); wicked problems (attack the assumptions of the model). <strong>Do not confuse with rational choice:</strong> rational comprehensive planning = a <strong>normative ideal</strong> where only the <strong>planner</strong> is rational; rational choice = an <strong>analytical</strong> theory where <strong>everyone</strong> is self interested and the state can fail too.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["The link with the Happy City (L1) is now explained (utilitarian: maximise total welfare, e.g. through CBA) instead of equating the two."]}, {"id": "2", "title": "Rational choice", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>THEORY</strong></p>\n<p><strong>2</strong></p></td>\n<td><h3>Rational choice</h3>\n<p>All actors (residents, landowners, developers, politicians, municipalities) <strong>act rationally to serve their self interest</strong>, weighing costs and benefits. Planning outcomes are the result of these choices and of the <strong>incentives</strong> created by rules such as property rights. Not only markets fail: <strong>governments fail</strong> too.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 Recognize it: signal words and typical article</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> self interest, incentives, costs and benefits, utility, strategic behaviour, rent seeking, free riding, collective action problem, externality, transaction costs, property rights, game, prisoner’s dilemma, principal agent, lobbying, NIMBY, market and state failure, efficiency, betterment, value capture.</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> explains why actors (landowners, homeowners, developers) behave as they do; models a \"game\"; analyses incentives created by a policy; a policy fails because actors respond strategically.</p>\n<p><strong>• Tell:</strong> behaviour is explained from <strong>individual interests</strong>; preferences are given, not explained.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 / Q2 Building blocks (name at least 3)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Homo economicus:</strong> actors maximize their own utility (not only money).</p>\n<p><strong>• Market failure and state failure:</strong> externalities, public goods; but officials also pursue their own interests.</p>\n<p><strong>• Collective action (Olson, Hindmoor):</strong> large groups fail because of <strong>free riding</strong>; small, concentrated interests organize better (selective incentives).</p>\n<p><strong>• Property rights (Needham):</strong> the state creates and can change rights; they shape incentives. The double role of the state.</p>\n<p><strong>• Rent seeking (Taylor):</strong> landowners lobby to capture unearned value (betterment); insiders vs outsiders.</p>\n<p><strong>• Coase:</strong> with clear rights and low transaction costs, actors bargain to an efficient outcome; goods: excludable / rival.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Framework dimension</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Position</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Write it like this in Q1 / Q2</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td>Agency</td>\n<td>\"Outcomes are explained by individual choices of self interested actors, e.g. landowners lobbying for higher land value.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reproduction ↔ transformation</strong></td>\n<td>Both</td>\n<td>\"Insiders use their position to protect the status quo (reproduction), while rules and incentives can be redesigned (transformation).\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deliberate ↔ emergent</strong></td>\n<td>Emergent outcomes, deliberate rule design</td>\n<td>\"Collective outcomes emerge from many individual choices, but the rules of the game can be changed on purpose.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Universal ↔ situated</strong></td>\n<td>Universal</td>\n<td>\"Assumes one universal model of human behaviour that applies in every context.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monist ↔ pluralist</strong></td>\n<td>Monist</td>\n<td>\"Knowledge comes from general models of behaviour built by the researcher (games, incentives); the actors' own interpretations are not needed.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Realism ↔ relativism</strong></td>\n<td>Realism (positivist)</td>\n<td>\"Behaviour can be modelled and predicted; claims can be tested.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normative</strong></td>\n<td>Efficiency</td>\n<td>\"Good outcomes are efficient outcomes; distribution and power remain implicit.\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q2 Use it as the alternative</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Best against:</strong> political economy (classes instead of individuals), urban politics (power and inequality), discourse (preferences are <strong>produced</strong>, not given), sociological institutionalism (logic of appropriateness instead of calculation).</p>\n<p><strong>• Use it as the alternative when</strong> an article explains everything by structure or discourse: rational choice reveals the <strong>concrete incentives and strategies</strong> of actors and why cooperation fails or succeeds (free riding, bargaining).</p>\n<p><strong>• Ready sentence:</strong> \"Where the author explains [X] through structural forces, a rational choice perspective assumes that outcomes result from the strategic choices of self interested actors, which reveals how incentives such as … shape behaviour.\"</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q3 Research focus and methods</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Typical RQ:</strong> \"Which incentives explain why [actors] support or block [policy]?\" or \"How do property rules affect the strategies of landowners?\"</p>\n<p><strong>• Methods:</strong> game theory and models, analysis of incentives and pay offs, surveys on preferences, statistical analysis of behaviour (prices, votes), analysis of submissions and lobbying (Taylor: 264 submissions), comparing rule designs.</p>\n<p><strong>• Why:</strong> actors are assumed to be rational and comparable, so behaviour can be <strong>modelled and measured</strong> from outside (etic).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Course examples to cite, and do not confuse with</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cite:</strong> Needham (property rights as policy tools, the staircase of land value, regulatory takings); Hindmoor on Olson (logic of collective action, Amnesty critique); Taylor (Melbourne growth boundary and GAIC; rent seeking, insiders vs outsiders); Airbnb example (Coase). <strong>Critiques:</strong> too simple a view of people, ignores power and inequality, only agency, unrealistic assumptions, \"nirvana fallacy in reverse\". <strong>Do not confuse with</strong> rational comprehensive planning (see sheet 1).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["Monist ↔ pluralist is about knowledge (who can produce it, which forms count), not about using one explanation. The rational choice row now says that."]}, {"id": "3", "title": "New institutionalism: the umbrella", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>THEORY</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong></p></td>\n<td><h3>New institutionalism: the umbrella</h3>\n<p><strong>Institutions matter.</strong> Institutions are the formal and informal <strong>\"rules of the game\"</strong> (North) that shape how people behave. Planning both <strong>works within</strong> institutions and tries to <strong>change</strong> them: planning itself is a form of <strong>institutional design</strong>. New institutionalism is not one theory but a <strong>family of four</strong>, which disagree on what institutions are, how people relate to them and how they change (Sorensen 2017).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>What all four share</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Institutions = rules of the game</strong>, not the players. Players are organizations and actors (North).</p>\n<p><strong>• Formal</strong> rules (laws, zoning plans, property rights, contracts) and <strong>informal</strong> rules (norms, routines, habits, shared meanings).</p>\n<p><strong>•</strong> Institutions both <strong>constrain and enable</strong> behaviour: they are the <strong>bridge between structure and agency</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>• \"New\" vs old institutionalism:</strong> the old version described formal government organizations; the new one (since the 1980s) asks how rules, <strong>including informal ones</strong>, shape behaviour and outcomes.</p>\n<p><strong>• For planning:</strong> land policy, property regimes and planning systems are institutions; planners ask whether they can <strong>design</strong> them (Innes, Alexander) or only change them modestly (Moroni).</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Recognize new institutionalism (any type)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> institutions, rules of the game, formal and informal rules, norms, routines, rules in use, governance arrangement, institutional design, institutional change, path dependence, commons, legitimacy, embeddedness.</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> explains why a planning system or policy <strong>stays the same</strong> or <strong>changes</strong>, how <strong>rules</strong> shape the behaviour of actors, or how a group <strong>governs a shared resource</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>• Tell:</strong> the explanation runs through <strong>rules and their working</strong>, not through classes (political economy), conflict (urban politics) or language (discourse).</p>\n<p><strong>• Then ask:</strong> which of the four types? Use the quick test below.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Rational choice (3a)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Sociological (3b)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Historical (3c)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Evolutionary (3d)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>What are institutions?</strong></td>\n<td>\"Humanly devised constraints\" (North)</td>\n<td>Frames of meaning: symbols, scripts, moral templates</td>\n<td>Procedures, routines, norms embedded in organizations and the political economy</td>\n<td>Rules that grew over time, not designed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>How do people relate to them?</strong></td>\n<td>External: actors calculate and adjust strategy</td>\n<td>Internal: people follow them because it feels appropriate</td>\n<td>Shaped by the past: earlier choices limit later ones</td>\n<td>\"Rule following animals\" using knowledge stored in rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>What do they do?</strong></td>\n<td>Give certainty, information, credible commitment; enable cooperation</td>\n<td>Shape what people want and give meaning</td>\n<td>Create stability; distribute benefits unequally</td>\n<td>Coordinate actions; store the knowledge of generations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>How do they change?</strong></td>\n<td>Actors design rules to improve cooperation</td>\n<td>Through daily practice, symbols and new habits</td>\n<td>Path dependence and critical junctures</td>\n<td>Slow trial and error: variation, selection, spread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td>Agency</td>\n<td>Both</td>\n<td>Leans structure</td>\n<td>Many dispersed actors acting within general rules; no designer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Key authors / reading</strong></td>\n<td>North, Ostrom, Williamson (link L5)</td>\n<td>March &amp; Olsen, DiMaggio &amp; Powell; Dembski &amp; Salet</td>\n<td>Hall &amp; Taylor, Pierson, Thelen; Sorensen</td>\n<td>Hayek, Moroni; Cozzolino</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Quick test: which type is it?</strong></p>\n<p>Actors <strong>calculate</strong> and <strong>design</strong> rules to cooperate or solve a commons problem → <strong>rational choice (3a)</strong>. Rules work through <strong>meaning, symbols, legitimacy</strong>, \"what is appropriate\" → <strong>sociological (3b)</strong>. The story is about <strong>history</strong>, lock in, a <strong>critical juncture</strong>, unequal benefits → <strong>historical (3c)</strong>. Order <strong>grows</strong> without a designer, <strong>limits of knowledge</strong>, general rules → <strong>evolutionary (3d)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip for Q1/Q2:</strong> name the <strong>type</strong>, not only \"new institutionalism\". Two types can be good opponents for each other in Q2 (design vs evolution: 3a vs 3d; calculation vs appropriateness: 3a vs 3b).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["The structure ↔ agency cell for evolutionary institutionalism said \"emergent\", which is a position on deliberate ↔ emergent. Now it describes agency within general rules."]}, {"id": "3a", "title": "Rational choice institutionalism", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3a</strong></td>\n<td><p><strong>Rational choice institutionalism</strong></p>\n<p>Actors are <strong>rational and self interested</strong>; institutions are <strong>\"humanly devised constraints\"</strong> that reduce uncertainty and transaction costs. Actors can <strong>design rules</strong> to solve collective action problems: a commons does not have to end in tragedy (<strong>Ostrom</strong>).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 Recognize it</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> rules in use, design principles, commons, self governance, collective action, free riding, monitoring, sanctions, transaction costs, incentives, credible commitment, property regime, IAD framework.</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> how a group governs a shared resource, which rule designs make cooperation work or fail.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Building blocks and authors</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• North:</strong> institutions vs organizations; rules give certainty and information.</p>\n<p><strong>• Ostrom:</strong> users can govern commons themselves; <strong>IAD framework</strong> with seven types of rules (lecture): position, boundary, choice, aggregation, information, payoff and scope rules. Ostrom (1990) also proposed principles for robust commons, which Bossuyt &amp; D’Ottaviano discuss (and warn may depoliticise housing commons).</p>\n<p><strong>• Williamson:</strong> transaction costs.</p>\n<p><strong>• Link L5:</strong> Olson, Hardin’s tragedy of the commons.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Agency</strong></td>\n<td>Rules matter, but actors create and use them strategically.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Change</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Deliberate design</strong></td>\n<td>Actors redesign rules to improve cooperation (transformation through design); good rules then stabilize.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Knowledge</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Universal, monist, realist</strong></td>\n<td>Positivist: general models of rational behaviour, game theory.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normative</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Efficiency, cooperation</strong></td>\n<td>Good rules produce efficient, lasting cooperation.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q2 As the alternative</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Against:</strong> political economy (rules serve capital), urban politics (conflict, not cooperation), sociological (calculation vs appropriateness).</p>\n<p><strong>• Reveals</strong> which concrete rules make an arrangement durable (e.g. Bossuyt &amp; D’Ottaviano: the points system works against free riding; the subletting ban fails without enforcement).</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q3 Research focus and methods</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• RQ:</strong> \"Which rules in use make [commons / arrangement] robust, and how are they monitored and enforced?\"</p>\n<p><strong>• Methods:</strong> IAD framework, analysis of statutes and contracts, interviews on rules in use, comparing successful and failed cases, games / experiments. <strong>Why:</strong> behaviour follows from rules and incentives, so map them.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["The NI lecture teaches Ostrom through the IAD framework and its seven rule types, not the eight design principles (those are not on the slides; Bossuyt & D'Ottaviano only mention Ostrom's principles). Replaced with the lecture version."]}, {"id": "3b", "title": "Sociological institutionalism", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3b</strong></td>\n<td><p><strong>Sociological institutionalism</strong></p>\n<p>Institutions are <strong>frames of meaning</strong> (symbols, scripts, moral templates). People follow them because it feels <strong>appropriate</strong>, not because they calculate. Institutions shape <strong>what people want</strong>: \"they establish the very criteria by which people discover their preferences\" (slide quote, 1991).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 Recognize it</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> norms, values, culture, meaning, symbols, scripts, legitimacy, logic of appropriateness, taken for granted, isomorphism, copying, templates, identity, shared understandings.</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> why a planning concept or symbol (Green Heart) keeps support; why organizations copy policies.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Building blocks and authors</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• March &amp; Olsen:</strong> logic of <strong>appropriateness</strong> vs logic of consequences.</p>\n<p><strong>• DiMaggio &amp; Powell (1983):</strong> <strong>goodness of fit</strong>: arrangements are reproduced because they fit established templates and are seen as legitimate (lecture slide).</p>\n<p><strong>• Savini &amp; Dembski (2016):</strong> the symbols of Amsterdam-Noord (shipyard past, creative future) mobilised consensus for a new development agenda (lecture example).</p>\n<p><strong>•</strong> Norms can be <strong>hegemonic</strong> (dominant, taken for granted).</p>\n<p><strong>• Dembski &amp; Salet:</strong> symbols (Green Heart, Green Belt) give institutions <strong>transformative potential</strong>.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Both</strong></td>\n<td>Norms are internalized (structure) but reset and changed in daily action (agency).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Change</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Mostly reproduction; gradual</strong></td>\n<td>Change through practice, new habits and new symbols.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Knowledge</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Situated, interpretive</strong></td>\n<td>Meaning depends on context; the researcher interprets shared understandings.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normative</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Social meaning, legitimacy</strong></td>\n<td>Values are part of the institution itself.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q2 As the alternative</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Against:</strong> rational choice and RCI (appropriateness, not calculation; preferences are shaped), political economy (meaning, not only money).</p>\n<p><strong>• Reveals</strong> why actors follow norms even against their interests, and the role of symbols. <strong>Not discourse:</strong> SI takes norms as given; discourse asks how they are made and contested.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q3 Research focus and methods</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• RQ:</strong> \"Which shared norms and symbols make actors support [concept], and how do they change in practice?\"</p>\n<p><strong>• Methods:</strong> interviews, participant observation in organizations, analysis of plans, images and symbols, comparing cases of copying. <strong>Why:</strong> norms are internalized, so they must be interpreted from inside.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["\"Isomorphism\" is not in the course material; the lecture uses DiMaggio & Powell's \"goodness of fit\". Added the lecture example Savini & Dembski (2016), Amsterdam-Noord.", "Source added for the sociological institutionalism quote (NI lecture slide, 1991)."]}, {"id": "3c", "title": "Historical institutionalism", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3c</strong></td>\n<td><p><strong>Historical institutionalism</strong></p>\n<p>Institutions are procedures and routines embedded in organizations and the political economy. <strong>Earlier choices limit later ones (path dependence)</strong>; real change comes at <strong>critical junctures</strong>. Institutions create stability but <strong>distribute benefits unequally</strong>.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 Recognize it</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> path dependence, lock in, increasing returns, critical juncture, sequence, timing, legacy, layering, drift, conversion, conjuncture, unequal benefits.</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> why a planning system or policy persists for decades, or changes at a moment of crisis; long time span told in phases.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Building blocks and authors</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Path dependence</strong> (Pierson: increasing returns).</p>\n<p><strong>• Critical junctures:</strong> short moments where change is possible.</p>\n<p><strong>• Gradual change</strong> (Thelen): layering, conversion, drift.</p>\n<p><strong>• Power:</strong> rules favour some groups.</p>\n<p><strong>• Sorensen:</strong> Toronto land value capture; conjunctural analysis (Gramsci, Stuart Hall); \"growth pays for growth\".</p>\n<p><strong>• Holsen (2021):</strong> lecture example: why public participation in Norwegian land-use planning stayed weak despite decades of legislation (a path-dependent explanation).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Leans structure</strong></td>\n<td>The past and existing rules shape what actors can do now.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Change</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Reproduction + junctures</strong></td>\n<td>Mostly reproduction (lock in); transformation at critical junctures; emergent over time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Knowledge</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Situated, realist</strong></td>\n<td>Explanations depend on the specific history of a place.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normative</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Often equity</strong></td>\n<td>Attention to who gains from rules (Sorensen).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q2 As the alternative</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Against:</strong> RCI (history and power, not free design), urban politics (stability, not agency), rational comprehensive planning (plans meet legacies).</p>\n<p><strong>• Reveals</strong> why change is slow and why good designs fail (e.g. why Brazil lacks collective property regimes in Bossuyt &amp; D’Ottaviano).</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q3 Research focus and methods</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• RQ:</strong> \"How did path dependence and critical junctures shape [policy] since [year]?\"</p>\n<p><strong>• Methods:</strong> <strong>process tracing</strong>, historical and archival document analysis, timelines, interviews with long serving actors, comparative historical analysis. <strong>Why:</strong> the explanation lies in sequences over time.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["Added the lecture example for historical institutionalism: Holsen (2021), Norwegian participation."]}, {"id": "3d", "title": "Evolutionary institutionalism", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3d</strong></td>\n<td><p><strong>Evolutionary institutionalism</strong></p>\n<p>Most institutions <strong>grew through slow, unplanned evolution</strong> (spontaneous order) and store the knowledge of generations that no planner can hold. Planners should therefore set <strong>general, abstract rules</strong> and be <strong>modest</strong>, not design outcomes.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 Recognize it</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> spontaneous order, kosmos vs taxis, evolution, trial and error, complexity, emergence, adaptive, self organization, tacit knowledge, limits of knowledge, general rules, proscriptive rules.</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> argues against master plans and for flexible frameworks or bottom up development (Oosterwold).</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Building blocks and authors</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Hayek:</strong> dispersed knowledge; spontaneous order; rule of law.</p>\n<p><strong>• Moroni:</strong> kosmos vs taxis; planning as institutional design, but modest; keep proven rules (property, contract).</p>\n<p><strong>• Cozzolino:</strong> complex adaptive systems; proscriptive (forbid harm) vs prescriptive rules; anti adaptive neighbourhoods.</p>\n<p><strong>• Jacobs (via Scott):</strong> order from many uncoordinated uses of the street (\"eyes on the street\").</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Emergent order of many agents</strong></td>\n<td>Many small actors act within general rules; no single designer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Change</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Emergent, gradual</strong></td>\n<td>Trial and error: variation, selection, spread.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Knowledge</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Dispersed, tacit, pluralist</strong></td>\n<td>Deep ignorance: no one can know enough to plan the whole.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normative</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Liberty, modesty</strong></td>\n<td>Close to the Free City (L1).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q2 As the alternative</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Against:</strong> rational comprehensive planning and high modernism (limits of knowledge), RCI (rules cannot simply be designed), political economy.</p>\n<p><strong>• Reveals</strong> unintended consequences and why designed places fail to adapt. <strong>Not HI:</strong> both are slow, but HI stresses lock in and power, EI sees evolution as useful selection.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q3 Research focus and methods</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• RQ:</strong> \"How did [area] change over time through many small actions, and which rules enabled or blocked adaptation?\"</p>\n<p><strong>• Methods:</strong> long term historical and morphological analysis, mapping change over time, comparing designed and grown areas, analysing rule types, complexity models. <strong>Why:</strong> order emerges over time from many actors.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["\"The invisible hand of the street\" is not a phrase from the sources; replaced with Jacobs' \"eyes on the street\" as discussed in Scott."]}, {"id": "4", "title": "Political economy", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>THEORY</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong></p></td>\n<td><h3>Political economy</h3>\n<p>Planning is part of <strong>capitalist state activity</strong>. The state ultimately serves the <strong>stability of capitalism</strong>, which is inherently <strong>prone to crisis</strong>, and planning works to <strong>ease these crises</strong> and to create new opportunities for <strong>capital accumulation</strong> (H1 to H3).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 Recognize it: signal words and typical article</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> capital accumulation, capitalism, class, surplus value, crisis, overaccumulation, capital switching, spatial fix, built environment, commodification, exchange value vs use value, rent gap, financialization, neoliberal(ization), entrepreneurial city, inter urban competition, growth coalition / growth machine, privatization, public private partnership, asset, investors.</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> links a local project or policy (land sales, waterfront, regeneration, housing reform) to <strong>wider economic forces</strong> and asks <strong>who profits</strong>; long time span; critical of inequality.</p>\n<p><strong>• Tell:</strong> the explanation goes back to <strong>capital and class</strong>, not to individual choices or discourse.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 / Q2 Building blocks (name at least 3)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Marx:</strong> class (workers vs capitalists), surplus value, the economy shapes politics and ideas.</p>\n<p><strong>• Crisis:</strong> overaccumulation and underconsumption.</p>\n<p><strong>• Capital switching / spatial fix (Harvey):</strong> surplus capital flows into the built environment.</p>\n<p><strong>• Four roles of planning:</strong> social reproduction (Castells, collective consumption); conditions of production; devaluation and revaluation; new accumulation.</p>\n<p><strong>• Contradictions (Foglesong):</strong> land is social but privately owned; capitalism vs democracy.</p>\n<p><strong>• Entrepreneurialism and neoliberalization (Harvey 1989; Peck):</strong> PPP, speculation, place not territory; roll back and roll out.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Framework dimension</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Position</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Write it like this in Q1 / Q2</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td>Structure</td>\n<td>\"The author explains [X] through the logic of capital accumulation; actors are carriers of this logic (inter-urban competition as an 'external coercive law', Harvey 1989).\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reproduction ↔ transformation</strong></td>\n<td>Reproduction (transformation via crisis)</td>\n<td>\"Planning helps capitalism reproduce itself; big shifts come from crisis.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deliberate ↔ emergent</strong></td>\n<td>Emergent</td>\n<td>\"The shift was not designed by anyone; it follows from crisis and competition, across political parties.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Universal ↔ situated</strong></td>\n<td>Universal (leans)</td>\n<td>\"The same logic of capital is found in Baltimore, Helsinki and Rotterdam, though shaped by context.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monist ↔ pluralist</strong></td>\n<td>Monist (leans)</td>\n<td>\"Theory-led analysis of capital and class is privileged over the actors' own accounts.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Realism ↔ relativism</strong></td>\n<td>Realism (critical realism)</td>\n<td>\"There is a real economic structure under the surface that the researcher uncovers.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normative</strong></td>\n<td>Equality, use value, justice</td>\n<td>\"Critical of inequality and commodification; sides with use value and the working class (Equal City).\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q2 Use it as the alternative</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Best against:</strong> rational choice (individuals, not classes), new institutionalism (rules as neutral), discourse (meaning instead of material interest), rational comprehensive planning (neutral public interest).</p>\n<p><strong>• Use it as the alternative when</strong> the article looks at rules, frames or participation without asking <strong>who gains</strong>: it reveals the <strong>money flows, economic interests</strong> and why policies keep returning (e.g. deregulation in the next downturn).</p>\n<p><strong>• Ready sentence:</strong> \"Where the author treats [rules / discourses] as the main explanation, political economy assumes that planning is shaped by the needs of capital accumulation, which reveals who profits from …\"</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q3 Research focus and methods</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Typical RQ:</strong> \"How does [project/policy] create opportunities for capital accumulation, and who profits and who loses?\"</p>\n<p><strong>• Methods:</strong> longitudinal / historical case study; following the money (land prices, investment flows, budgets, subsidies, ownership data); policy and document analysis; interviews with elite actors (officials, developers, investors); linking local events to macro economic shifts.</p>\n<p><strong>• Why:</strong> the theory sees the explanation in <strong>economic structures over time</strong>, so you need data on <strong>capital and ownership</strong> (etic view).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Course examples to cite, and do not confuse with</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cite:</strong> Harvey 1989 (Baltimore, managerialism to entrepreneurialism, \"bread and circuses\"); Foglesong 2003 (\"While capitalism engenders demands for market intervention, it also constrains the realization of these demands\", p. 3); Hyötyläinen &amp; Haila 2018 (Eiranranta, selling public land, good taxpayers); Kop van Zuid. Exam examples: <strong>Lima 2025</strong> (political economy, with Harvey). <strong>Do not confuse with urban politics:</strong> political economy = politics follows the economy; urban politics = conflict has a <strong>degree of autonomy</strong>.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["Harvey's phrase is \"external coercive law\" (of competition).", "Monist ↔ pluralist row for political economy rewritten in terms of knowledge (see sheet 2)."]}, {"id": "5", "title": "Urban politics", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>THEORY</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5</strong></p></td>\n<td><h3>Urban politics</h3>\n<p><strong>Planning is political:</strong> conflict is unavoidable in process and outcome. Conflict can be analysed through <strong>power and inequality</strong>, it has a <strong>degree of autonomy</strong> from socio economic structures, and planners should ask <strong>how to plan justly</strong> (they cannot be neutral).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 Recognize it: signal words and typical article</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> conflict, power, inequality, marginalized, dispossessed, contestation, struggle, protest, occupation, mobilization, social movement, activists, coalition, urban regime, growth coalition, agonism, post political, right to the city, environmental justice, cooptation, institutionalization, opportunity structure, grassroots, emancipation.</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> a case of <strong>conflict or mobilization</strong> (movement, protest, occupation, coalition); voices of residents; who wins and who is excluded; qualitative, close to the people studied.</p>\n<p><strong>• Tell:</strong> the actors are <strong>groups with unequal power</strong>, and the author cares about <strong>justice</strong>.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 / Q2 Building blocks (name at least 3)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Three faces of power (Lukes):</strong> decision making (Dahl), agenda setting / non decisions (Bachrach &amp; Baratz), ideological (shaping preferences).</p>\n<p><strong>• Autonomy of politics (Gramsci):</strong> change short of revolution, through people’s action, incrementally.</p>\n<p><strong>• Urban social movements (Castells):</strong> collective consumption, identity, control over space.</p>\n<p><strong>• Urban regimes (Stone) and growth coalitions (Molotch):</strong> informal, stable coalitions that crowd out other agendas.</p>\n<p><strong>• Agonism (Mouffe):</strong> conflict is productive; adversaries, not enemies.</p>\n<p><strong>• Justice (Agyeman):</strong> recognition, process, procedures, outcomes.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Framework dimension</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Position</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Write it like this in Q1 / Q2</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td>Duality, leans agency</td>\n<td>\"Movements act strategically within structures that enable and constrain them (strategic selectivity).\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reproduction ↔ transformation</strong></td>\n<td>Transformation (incremental)</td>\n<td>\"Change is possible through mobilization and reform, though power also reproduces inequality.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deliberate ↔ emergent</strong></td>\n<td>Deliberate (leans)</td>\n<td>\"Change comes from the strategic actions of movements and coalitions.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Universal ↔ situated</strong></td>\n<td>Situated</td>\n<td>\"Knowledge depends on position; the perspective of the marginalized is central (emic).\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monist ↔ pluralist</strong></td>\n<td>Pluralist</td>\n<td>\"Residents’ and activists’ knowledge counts, not only expert knowledge.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Realism ↔ relativism</strong></td>\n<td>Realism (leans)</td>\n<td>\"Power and inequality are real and claims about justice can be judged.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normative</strong></td>\n<td>Justice, equity, equality</td>\n<td>\"The author is sympathetic to [movement]; values like the right to housing are present, even if not declared.\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q2 Use it as the alternative</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Best against:</strong> political economy (autonomy of conflict instead of structural determination), rational comprehensive planning and governance (no neutrality, no easy consensus), rational choice and RCI (power and inequality instead of efficiency), discourse (identifiable groups hold power).</p>\n<p><strong>• Use it as the alternative when</strong> the article treats actors as passive, the state as neutral, or rules as efficient: it reveals <strong>who is excluded</strong>, which issues <strong>never reach the agenda</strong>, and how affected groups <strong>mobilize and resist</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>• Ready sentence:</strong> \"Where the author assumes [structure / neutral rules], urban politics assumes that conflict has a degree of autonomy and that power is unequally distributed, which reveals …\"</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q3 Research focus and methods</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Typical RQ:</strong> \"How do [affected groups] experience, contest and try to change [policy], and who is excluded from the decision?\"</p>\n<p><strong>• Methods:</strong> qualitative and <strong>emic</strong>: in depth interviews, focus groups, participant observation, <strong>ethnography</strong>, <strong>participatory action research</strong>; network analysis of coalitions; process sequencing; comparative case studies (Pruijt).</p>\n<p><strong>• Why:</strong> what matters is how people <strong>perceive</strong> their situation and <strong>act</strong> on it, which you cannot know from outside (the resident who supplies the counterfactual herself).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Course examples to cite, and do not confuse with</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cite:</strong> Rydin Ch. 6 (\"planners cannot be neutral technocrats or bureaucrats but need to behave more like advocates\", p. 105; politics \"not reduced to being a handmaiden\" of structures, p. 110); Bossuyt &amp; D’Ottaviano 2025 (São Paulo occupations, five patterns of protection); Pruijt 2003 (squatting, cooptation vs flexible institutionalization); tenants and the three faces. Exam example: <strong>Lima 2025</strong> commentary used <strong>urban politics</strong> as the alternative with <strong>emic ethnography</strong>. <strong>Do not confuse</strong> with pluralism: pluralists (Dahl) see power as fragmented and dispersed among groups in formal politics; urban politics sees conflict framed by unequal relations between groups, and politics also outside formal institutions (protest, direct action) (HC urban politics slides 22, 43).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["\"Cumulative\" power is not in the sources; the pluralism contrast now follows the urban politics slides (22, 43)."]}, {"id": "6", "title": "Discourse, power and knowledge", "html": "<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>THEORY</strong></p>\n<p><strong>6</strong></p></td>\n<td><h3>Discourse, power and knowledge</h3>\n<p>How we <strong>talk about, frame and know</strong> issues matters: discourses stabilize certain <strong>\"truths\"</strong> and marginalize others. Power is <strong>relational, circulating and productive</strong>, works through <strong>knowledge</strong> (power/knowledge) and shapes how people <strong>govern themselves</strong> (governmentality).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 Recognize it: signal words and typical article</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Words:</strong> discourse, narrative, storyline, frame, framing, rhetoric, representation, construction, \"common sense\", taken for granted, regime of truth, hegemony, problematization, rationality, governmentality, conduct of conduct, technologies of government, responsibilization, active citizen, subjectivity, calculative practices, performativity, expertise, discourse coalition.</p>\n<p><strong>• Typical article:</strong> analyses <strong>texts</strong> (policy documents, submissions, media, speeches) to show how a problem is <strong>framed</strong> and whose views dominate; or how a policy <strong>shapes subjects</strong> (responsible citizens).</p>\n<p><strong>• Tell:</strong> language and knowledge are treated as <strong>producing</strong> reality, not just describing it.</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q1 / Q2 Building blocks (name at least 3)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Discourse and framing:</strong> problems are socially constructed (housing crisis: politicizing vs depoliticizing frames).</p>\n<p><strong>• Power/knowledge (Foucault):</strong> who controls knowledge defines what is true, rational, feasible.</p>\n<p><strong>• Regime of truth:</strong> dominant version of reality setting acceptable problems and solutions.</p>\n<p><strong>• Governmentality:</strong> conduct of conduct; governing <strong>through</strong> the governed; <strong>technologies of government</strong> (benchmarks, audits, capacity building).</p>\n<p><strong>• Discourse coalitions (Hajer):</strong> storyline → coalition → structuration → institutionalization.</p>\n<p><strong>• Calculative devices (Murphy):</strong> a valuation can <strong>produce</strong> the market it claims to describe.</p></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Framework dimension</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Position</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Write it like this in Q1 / Q2</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structure ↔ agency</strong></td>\n<td>Both</td>\n<td>\"Discourses constrain and enable actors, but actors also produce and contest them\" (Foucault leans structure; Hajer leans agency).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reproduction ↔ transformation</strong></td>\n<td>Reproduction (leans)</td>\n<td>\"Repeated language makes ideas common sense; but constructed truths can be rearticulated.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deliberate ↔ emergent</strong></td>\n<td>Emergent</td>\n<td>\"No single actor designs a discourse, though actors use storylines strategically.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Universal ↔ situated</strong></td>\n<td>Situated</td>\n<td>\"Knowledge is produced within specific power relations, times and places.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monist ↔ pluralist</strong></td>\n<td>Pluralist</td>\n<td>\"Many competing knowledges and discourses; attention to marginalized ones.\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Realism ↔ relativism</strong></td>\n<td>Relativism (soft constructivism)</td>\n<td>\"What counts as true depends on discourse: language is central in 'producing or constructing reality' (Gurran &amp; Ruming, p. 2 of the course PDF).\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normative</strong></td>\n<td>Critical, often implicit</td>\n<td>\"Unmasks what looks neutral; sympathetic to silenced voices and democratic debate.\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Q2 Use it as the alternative</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Best against:</strong> rational comprehensive planning and evidence based studies (knowledge is not neutral), rational choice (preferences are produced), political economy (power circulates, meaning matters), sociological institutionalism (how norms are made).</p>\n<p><strong>• Use it as the alternative when</strong> the article takes a category for granted (vulnerability, viability, affordability, community): it reveals <strong>how the problem was framed</strong>, <strong>whose knowledge counts</strong>, and what the framing makes invisible.</p>\n<p><strong>• Template:</strong> \"The authors assume [X] can be understood by … While this helps planners …, it overlooks … A discursive perspective would allow us to …\"</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Q3 Research focus and methods</strong></p>\n<p><strong>• Typical RQ:</strong> \"How was [problem] framed in policy documents and media, which storylines dominated, and whose voices were marginalized?\"</p>\n<p><strong>• Methods:</strong> <strong>discourse analysis</strong> of documents, submissions, media, speeches; frame / storyline analysis (Hajer); <strong>genealogy</strong> of a concept; analysis of calculative devices and models; interviews read as <strong>narratives</strong>; coding (NVivo).</p>\n<p><strong>• Why:</strong> meaning and power are made <strong>in text and talk</strong>, so the material is language; the aim is to question categories, not to measure them.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Course examples to cite, and do not confuse with</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cite:</strong> Raco &amp; Imrie 2000 (SRB, governing through community; \"in return for a little extra freedom of action, subjects come under greater monitoring\", p. 2201); Gurran &amp; Ruming 2016 (NSW reform: planning framed as \"red tape\", industry narratives as regimes of truth); Murphy (residual valuation); Taylor revisited (the landowner’s storyline instead of rent seeking); Swiss tenants voting as homeowners. Exam example: <strong>Lima 2025</strong> (political economy + performativity / Foucauldian discourse). <strong>Do not confuse</strong> with SI (see sheet 3).</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": ["Gurran & Ruming quote: it is on p. 2 of the course PDF (online-first version); \"p. 264\" could not be confirmed (the journal issue runs pp. 262–280)."]}, {"id": "7", "title": "7. Exam tools", "html": "<h3>7. Exam tools</h3>\n<p><strong>Which alternative for which article? (Q2)</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Article theory</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Strong alternative</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Key contrast (dimension)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Method switch</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rational comprehensive / evidence based</strong></td>\n<td>Discourse or urban politics</td>\n<td>Universal, monist → situated, pluralist</td>\n<td>Models, indicators → discourse analysis or emic interviews</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rational choice</strong></td>\n<td>Political economy, discourse or SI</td>\n<td>Agency → structure; given → produced preferences</td>\n<td>Incentive models → money flows or discourse analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>New institutionalism (3a to 3d)</strong></td>\n<td>Political economy, urban politics, or another type of institutionalism</td>\n<td>Rules as neutral → rules serve capital / power; reproduction → transformation</td>\n<td>Institutional analysis → follow the money or ethnography</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Political economy</strong></td>\n<td>Urban politics or neo institutionalism</td>\n<td>Structure → duality / agency; emergent → deliberate</td>\n<td>Etic macro analysis → emic PAR / IAD and process tracing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Urban politics</strong></td>\n<td>Neo institutionalism (Ostrom) or political economy</td>\n<td>Conflict → rules and stability; agency → structure</td>\n<td>Ethnography → analysis of rules in use / ownership data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Discourse</strong></td>\n<td>Political economy or urban politics</td>\n<td>Meaning → material interests; circulating power → groups holding power</td>\n<td>Text analysis → money flows / interviews with groups</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Answer templates</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Q1 opening:</strong> \"The author examines [issue] in order to explain/argue [aim]. The article best fits a [theory] perspective, because [reason], as shown by: “…” (p. x) and “…” (p. y). Its main building blocks are …\"</p>\n<p><strong>Assumption:</strong> \"A first assumption concerns [dimension]: the author assumes [position], since [how it shows in the article]: “…” (p. z).\"</p>\n<p><strong>Q2 opening:</strong> \"An alternative is [theory]. Its building blocks are … On [dimension 1] it assumes … and on [dimension 2] … Where the author assumes …, this theory assumes … This is relevant here because … It helps us see …, which the original perspective overlooks.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Q3 opening:</strong> \"From a [theory] perspective, the research focus shifts from [old question] to [new question]. Fitting methods are …, because the theory assumes … For example, the researcher could …\"</p></td>\n<td><p><strong>Time plan (3 hours)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>0:00 to 0:10</strong> read both abstracts, choose.</p>\n<p><strong>0:10 to 0:55</strong> read the article, highlight signal words, mark 4 to 6 quotes with page numbers (2 for theory, 2 for assumptions, spare).</p>\n<p><strong>0:55 to 1:45</strong> Q1 (40 p).</p>\n<p><strong>1:45 to 2:35</strong> Q2 (40 p).</p>\n<p><strong>2:35 to 2:55</strong> Q3 (20 p).</p>\n<p><strong>2:55 to 3:00</strong> check: theory named? 2 quotes? 2 assumptions with quotes? 2 dimensions in Q2? methods with \"why\" and an example?</p>\n<p><strong>Highlighter code:</strong> yellow = theory signal words, green = assumptions, pink = methods, blue = values.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><p><strong>Checklist for top marks (from the example answers)</strong></p>\n<p>Explain the theory <strong>in your own words</strong> before the quote; the quote supports, it does not replace the explanation.</p>\n<p>Each assumption = <strong>dimension + position + how it shows in the article + quote</strong>. Two different dimensions.</p>\n<p>In Q2, <strong>compare explicitly</strong>: \"whereas …, this perspective …\". Say what becomes <strong>visible</strong> that was invisible.</p>\n<p>In Q3, make the new research question <strong>concrete</strong> (place, actors, material) and link each method to an <strong>assumption</strong> of the theory (e.g. emic because knowledge is situated).</p>\n<p>Stay <strong>analytical</strong>: no verdict on the policy, only on what each lens shows and hides.</p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n", "fix": []}];
